The PS1 variable in Linux is an environment variable that defines the appearance and structure of your primary command prompt in the terminal.
It stands for "Prompt String 1" and dictates what you see right before you type a command.

echo $PS1
[\u@\h \W]\$

\u ->username
@
\h -> hostname
\w ->pwd
$ ->user is root user
# ->user is normal user

Temporary Change
PS1="\u:\W \$ "

Permanent Change
nano ~/.bashrc
PS1="[\t] \u@\h:\w\$ "

source .bashrc

PS1="touch me :"
touch me! :

if you want to modify how your prompt string looks, like its color, content, you can edit the PS1 variable

