exported variables

• Shell Variable (Local): If you define a variable like MY_VAR="hello", it only exists within your current, exact terminal window. If you run a Python script or a bash script from that terminal, the script will not be able to see or use MY_VAR.

• Exported Variable (Global/Environment): If you use the export command (e.g., export MY_VAR="hello"), the variable enters the operating system's environment. 
(Note: Inheritance is strictly top-down. A child process can inherit an exported variable from its parent, but a child process cannot export a variable back up to its parent shell.)

Every program, command, or script you run from that terminal session will inherit it and be able to read its value.
