1. Combining it with sudo for Redirection (The most common use case)

sudo echo "127.0.0.1 mysite.local" > /etc/hosts   # ERROR: Permission denied

If you try to use sudo to write data to a system file using a redirect operator (>), it will fail.
Why it fails: Only the echo command runs as root. The > redirection is handled by your current standard user shell, which doesn't have permission to write to /etc/hosts.

The Fix: You use bash -c so that the entire string—both the echo and the writing to the file—happens inside a shell that has full root privileges:
sudo bash -c "echo '127.0.0.1 mysite.local' > /etc/hosts"  # WORKS PERFECTLY

The -c flag tells bash to execute the very next single string as a command.
bash -c echo $var1

it will not work as expected because, -c flag tells bash to execute the very next single string as a command. In this case, that string was just echo, 30 was treated as an extra positional argument passed
so, bash ran echo with no arguments, printing a blank line.

to make it execute correctly, pass the command as single string

bash -c 'echo $VAR1'



2. Running Linux Commands from Other Tools
If you are writing programs in languages like Python, Node.js, or configuring tools like Docker, Kubernetes, or Cron jobs, they often require you to pass your command so that linux will detect that command inside DockerFile, python etc..

In a Dockerfile:
RUN bash -c "apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl"

In a Python script:
