In Linux, a variable is delineated (isolated or wrapped) by surrounding its name with curly braces {} and a dollar sign $

It tells the shell exactly where the variable name ends and where surrounding text begins. Without braces, the shell gets confused by attached characters.

NAME="Linux"

# ❌ WRONG (The shell looks for a variable named $NAMEisgreat)
echo "$NAMEisgreat" 
# Output: [Blank]

#  RIGHT (The braces isolate the variable name)
echo "${NAME}isgreat"
# Output: Linuxisgreat

• Use $VARIABLE for standalone variables.
• Use ${VARIABLE} when you need to attach text, numbers, or underscores directly to the variable's value.

